Given \[x(nT)=
\begin{cases}
a^n, &amp; nT\geq 0,\\
0, &amp; nT&lt;0,
\end{cases}
\qquad a&gt;0,\] the sequence is a right-sided exponential sequence. Its \(z\)-transform is \[X(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a^n z^{-n}
=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(az^{-1})^n
=\frac{1}{1-az^{-1}}.\] For convergence, \[|az^{-1}|&lt;1
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
|z|&gt;a.\] Hence, \[\boxed{\text{(b)}\;\frac{1}{1-az^{-1}},\;|z|&gt;a}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A right-sided sequence has an ROC outside the outermost pole.</li><li>For \(a^n u[n]\), the \(z\)-transform is \(\displaystyle \frac{1}{1-az^{-1}}\) with ROC \(|z|&gt;a\).</li></ul></div>

For the same a.c. input voltage and load impedance: Average load current for a half-wave rectifier is \[I_{\text{dc(HWR)}}=\frac{I_{m}}{\pi}\] Average load current for a full-wave rectifier is \[I_{\text{dc(FWR)}}=\frac{2I_{m}}{\pi}\] Therefore, \(I_{\text{dc(FWR)}}=2I_{\text{dc(HWR)}}\) Hence statement (a) is correct. Full wave rectifier generally means Center tapped full wave rectifier which uses center tapped transformer which is bigger than a normal transformer. Hence statement (d) is correct. \[\boxed{\begin{array}{l}
\text{(A)}\quad \text{The average load current in a full wave rectifier is twice than in a half wave rectifier}\\
\text{(D)}\quad \text{Half wave rectifier will have a small sized transformer compared to full wave rectifier}
\end{array}}\]

For an ideal diode with ideality factor \(\eta=1\) the diode equation is \[I=I_{S}\left(e^{\dfrac{eV}{KT}}-1\right)\] where \(V=\text{voltage across the diode}\) Since the two diodes are connected in series, the same current flows through both diodes. Let the voltage drops across \(D_{1}\) and \(D_{2}\) be \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\), respectively. For diode \(D_{1}\), \[I=I_{S}\left(e^{\dfrac{eV_{1}}{KT}}-1\right)\] \[\frac{I}{I_{S}}+1=e^{\dfrac{eV_{1}}{KT}}\] \[e^{\dfrac{eV_{1}}{KT}}=\frac{I}{I_{S}}+1\] For diode \(D_{2}\), the current direction is opposite to its forward direction. Hence, \[I=I_{S}\left(1-e^{-\dfrac{eV_{2}}{KT}}\right)\] \[e^{-\dfrac{eV_{2}}{KT}}=1-\frac{I}{I_{S}}\] Adding the two expressions, \[e^{\dfrac{eV_{1}}{KT}}
+
e^{-\dfrac{eV_{2}}{KT}}
=
\left(\frac{I}{I_{S}}+1\right)
+
\left(1-\frac{I}{I_{S}}\right)\] \[\boxed{
e^{\dfrac{eV_{1}}{KT}}
+
e^{-\dfrac{eV_{2}}{KT}}
=
2}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use the same polarity expression for both diodes as they are conneted in opposite directions.</li></ul></div>

The Thevenin equivalent of the biasing network is <img src="img/c8709de8301ae266.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,160px)"> \[V_{Th}=V_{CC}\frac{R_1}{R_1+R_2},
\qquad
R_{Th}=R_1\parallel R_2=R_B\] Applying KVL to the input loop, \[V_{Th}=I_BR_B+V_{BE}+I_ER_E\] Since \(I_E=I_B+I_C,\) \[V_{Th}=I_BR_B+V_{BE}+(I_B+I_C)R_E\] Differentiating w.r.t. \(I_C\), keeping \(\beta\) and \(V_{BE}\) constant, \[0=(R_B+R_E)\frac{\partial I_B}{\partial I_C}+R_E\] \[\frac{\partial I_B}{\partial I_C}
=-\frac{R_E}{R_B+R_E}\] The stability factor is, \[S=\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta\dfrac{\partial I_B}{\partial I_C}}\] \[S=\frac{1+\beta}
{1+\beta\left(\dfrac{R_E}{R_B+R_E}\right)}\] Since \(\beta\gg1\), \[S\approx
1+\frac{R_B}{R_E}\] For better stability, \(S\approx1\) which requires \(\dfrac{R_B}{R_E}\ll1\) or equivalently, \[\frac{R_E}{R_B}\gg1\] \[\boxed{(B)\;\dfrac{R_E}{R_B}\gg1}\]
